{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00494 - MONDO_10482",
  "verbalization": "Entacapone, a catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) inhibitor, is implicated in the treatment of Hereditary Late Onset Parkinson Disease (HLOPD), a rare form of Parkinson’s disease. The evidence suggests that Entacapone may have a role in the pathophysiology of X-linked Parkinsonism-spasticity Syndrome (XPSP), a rare neurodegenerative disorder characterized by spasticity and parkinsonism. The observed indications for Entacapone in HLOPD, coupled with its association with XPSP, indicate a potential mechanistic link involving the modulation of dopamine metabolism. The pathways suggest that Entacapone may influence the progression of Parkinson’s disease through the inhibition of COMT, which is involved in the degradation of dopamine. This inhibition could lead to increased dopamine levels in the brain, potentially affecting the motor and non-motor symptoms of XPSP. The integration of these pathways highlights the possibility that Entacapone’s effects on dopamine metabolism may contribute to the pathogenesis of XPSP, though further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm this relationship.",
